  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "ES-C61-ni",
      "sequence": 2405,
      "title": "ni — joining two absences",
      "headword": "ni",
      "romanization": "ni",
      "gloss": "nor, not even — the joining word for two things that are both absent, and the one place Spanish stacks negatives on purpose",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:e9025e32e74b39a9",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Y joins two things you have. O offers a choice between them."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Now: neither one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart: ni",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "No quiero café ni agua. — I don't want coffee or water."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ni is Latin nec, and the important thing about it is that the no is inside the word. It does not mean or; it means and not."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So it is not the negative twin of y. It is a word that carries its own refusal, which is why it can start a phrase on its own: Ni café. — Not even coffee."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Look again at that sentence and count the negatives."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "No quiero café ni agua."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two — and it is correct. English was taught to treat two negatives as cancelling out; Spanish never agreed. Here they agree with each other instead: the sentence is negative, so every part of it that can be negative, is."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This is worth holding on to, because it will keep happening. Once a Spanish sentence turns negative, the negativity spreads rather than doubling back."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
